###### Abstract

We have investigated different scenarios for the origin of the binary millisecond pulsar PSR J1911-5958A in NGC6752, the most distant pulsar discovered from the core of a globular cluster to date. The hypothesis that it results from a truly primordial binary born in the halo calls for accretion-induced collapse and negligible recoil speed at the moment of neutron star formation. Scattering or exchange interactions off cluster stars are not consistent with both the observed orbital period and its offset position. We show that a binary system of two black holes with (unequal) masses in the range of 3-100M_{\\odot} can live in NGC6752 until present time and can have propelled PSR J1911-5958A into an eccentric peripheral orbit during the last \\sim 1 Gyr. black hole physics -- globular clusters: general -- globular cluster: individual (NGC 6752) -- pulsars: individual (PSR J1911-5958A) -- stars: neutron +

