This secures a [BH+BH] lifetime comparable to that of NGC6752, or slightly shorter.

### Psr-A Ejection

Let us now come to question (_ii_). In the ejection hypotheses, the binary [NS{}_{\\rm A}+co] must have been propelled into an eccentric peripheral orbit at a speed V_{\\rm ej}\\lesssim V_{\\rm esc}. The apocenter then reduces due to dynamical friction but its \\epsilon-folding time \\tau_{\\rm df}\\gtrsim7\\times 10^{8}(1.6M_{\\odot}/M) yr is sufficiently long to permit the detection of PSR-A during the pulsar life-time \\sim\\tau_{\\rm PSR-A}.

In the _scattering_ off [BH+BH], the light binary [NS{}_{\\rm A}+co] is viewed as a single point mass. In this case, [NS{}_{\\rm A}+co] has to avoid break up by the BH tidal field and this imposes a constraint on the distance of closest approach, r_{\\rm min}>r_{\\rm fid} where

r_{\\rm fid}\\simeq 0.2\\left(\\frac{M_{\\rm BH,i}}{100M_{\\odot}}\\frac{1.6M_{ \\odot}}{m_{\\rm PSR-A}+m_{\\rm c}}\\right)^{1/3}\\!\\!{\\rm AU}. 

